{
 "about": "The iPad host's draw and hold (docs/dev/ipad.md 'The pen', step 6; docs/dev/handwriting.md 'Client'): the rules the web (frontend/src/ink/shapes.js stillSince, followHold, refitShape) and the iPad shell (ipad/GammaIPad/Pencil: StrokeRecorder's stillSince, ShapeHold.current, HeldShape.refit) run over the same samples, so the shape the shell draws under the pen is the one the web commits. Every coordinate is in the host's viewport css px (= points), y down; a sample is [x, y, p, t] as the shell sends it (p the pressure 0..1, t integer ms from the first sample; a six-value sample has its altitude and azimuth after, and no rule here reads p, a or z); `from` and `pointer` are [x, y]. Constants: HOLD_MOVE 3, REFIT_PX 6, CANCEL_PX 12, CANCEL_MS 150 (HOLD_MS 600 only in how tSnap was made). STILL: `stillSince` is the stillness anchor of `samples`: start at index 0; each later sample more than HOLD_MOVE (strictly, Euclidean) from the anchor's sample becomes the anchor; the answer is the anchor's index. The shell's hold timer fires HOLD_MS after the anchor's t, so a hold's tSnap is samples[stillSince].t + 600. FOLLOW: `hold` is what the web keeps when it answers a hold with a shape: {kind, params (the snap, css px), from (the last sample the hold carried), n (how many it carried), tSnap}; `samples` is the whole stroke, its first n the ones the hold carried. Over samples[n...]: a sample whose distance from `from` is > CANCEL_PX while t - tSnap <= CANCEL_MS drops the shape (expect null: freehand comes back); otherwise, if any of them was >= REFIT_PX from `from`, the shape is REFIT (below) to the stroke's last sample, from `from`; else it is the snap's params unchanged. REFIT (shapes.js refitShape without Shift, always from the snap's params): a line or an arrow keeps x1, y1 (and an arrow its head) and takes the pointer as x2, y2; a closed shape scales about its centre (cx, cy; a triangle's centroid) by s = |pointer - centre| / |from - centre| and turns about it by the angle from `from` to `pointer` (degrees, atan2 in the y-down frame), s = 1 and no turn when |from - centre| <= 1e-6: a circle's r times s; a rect's w and h times s and angle + turn, normalised as a = mod(round2(angle) + 45, 90) - 45, w and h swapped when round((angle - a) / 90) is odd; a polygon's r times s and angle mod(round2(angle), 360 / n); an ellipse's rx, ry times s and angle mod(round2(angle), 180) (rx >= ry stays); a triangle's corners turned and scaled about its centroid; every number but n rounded to 0.01 as JavaScript's Math.round does (floor(v * 100 + 0.5) / 100), and mod(v, m) = ((v % m) + m) % m. Checks: every number within 0.011 (n exactly), the same keys, null exactly. frontend/tests/shapes.test.mjs runs every case; the iPad app's PencilTests run them through the Swift ports.",
